# Circles: Chords, Angles, and π

> A circle is the set of points at a fixed distance (the radius) from a
> centre. Its symmetry produces the cleanest angle rules in geometry.

## 1. Core Vocabulary

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| Radius | Centre to any point on the circle |
| Diameter | A chord through the centre — the longest one ($d = 2r$) |
| Chord | Segment joining two points on the circle |
| Tangent | Line touching the circle at exactly one point |
| Arc | Part of the circumference |
| Sector | "Pizza slice" between two radii |

## 2. The Formulas Everything Uses

@math label="eq:circle-formulas"
C = 2\pi r = \pi d, \qquad A = \pi r^2
@/math

## Example

A circle of radius $7$ cm has circumference $C = 2\pi \cdot 7 = 14\pi$
cm and area $\pi \cdot 7^2 = 49\pi$ cm². Leave answers in terms of $\pi$
unless asked otherwise.

## 3. Angle Rules Inside Circles

- The angle in a **semicircle is always 90°** (Thales' theorem).
- Angles from the same arc at the circumference are **equal**.
- The angle at the **centre** is **twice** the angle at the
  circumference standing on the same arc.

@diagram flowchart id="central-angle" layout="hierarchical" aria="Central angle versus inscribed angle"
node centre label="Centre: 80°" shape="circle"
node rim1 label="At rim, same arc: 40°" shape="round-rect"
node rim2 label="Other side of arc: also 40°" shape="round-rect"

centre -> rim1 label="double"
centre -> rim2 label="double"
@/diagram

[definition]
A **cyclic quadrilateral** has all four corners on one circle. Its
opposite angles are supplementary — they sum to 180°.
[/definition]

## 4. Tangent Facts

1. A tangent meets the radius at the touch point at exactly **90°**.
2. Two tangents drawn from an external point have **equal lengths**.

## Example

Two tangents from point P touch a circle at A and B with $PA = 6$ cm.
Then $PB$ is also $6$ cm — no calculation needed.

## 5. Sectors and Pie Thinking

A sector's share of the circle equals its angle's share of 360°:

@chart donut title="Sector shares of a full turn" labels:
120° sector
90° sector
150° remainder

data:
120
90
150
@/chart

A 120° sector therefore holds $\frac{120}{360} = \frac{1}{3}$ of the
circle's area.

## Key Takeaways

- $C = 2\pi r$, $A = \pi r^2$ — memorise cold.
- Centre angle = 2 × rim angle; semicircle angles are right angles.
- Tangents meet radii at 90° and come in equal pairs from a point.
